Why Are Architectural Membranes Being Specified?
Large-span buildings and outdoor public venues need lightweight coverage without sacrificing long-term durability and safety. Coated glass textiles can carry loads through a carefully engineered tensile system while diffusing daylight and limiting the mass of conventional roof assemblies. The membrane alone is not the structure. Cable forces, edge plates, steel supports, clamping and water drainage determine whether a roof behaves correctly under wind, heat and rain.

Selection also depends on surface finish, strength along fabric directions, fire certification and the appearance desired by architects. A glossy coating may respond differently to lighting and maintenance than a matte presentation. Each performance parameter has to be evaluated for the approved product grade and installation conditions, rather than inferred from broad claims about all fluoropolymers.

